  "notes": "Black Ops II, Steam app 202970, which Plutonium T6 runs on. D164: this pack now carries the game's aim model (aimModel, format zs-aim-model/1, docs/AIM-MODEL.md) so the Core's translator inverts BO2's own stick law instead of a generic linear guess. D172 (2026-10-01): the model is now MEASURED by the Core's auto-calibration on the owner's game (needsCalibration false); the sourced D164 model it replaces is kept in docs/calibration/bo2-sourced-model-d164.json. The owner's BO2 Plutonium calibration pack lives on his board, not in the repo, and D39 found one of its four numbers mislabelled, so it is not copied in. D394 (2026-10-15), amended the same day: owner 2026-10-15: BO2 uses BO7's values; our BO2 measurement is kept as an alternate. The aim translator (aimModel, aimVariants: Standard, Linear, Dynamic, the PlayStation and Xbox speeds, and \"Set these in the game\") is Black Ops 7's, number for number, imported from XIM's plaintext data by dashboard/tools/import-xim-translators.js exactly as Warzone's is; the record is dashboard/game-translators/cod-bo2.json. Our own measured BO2 translator (D172: a 16-point curve per mode, dead zone 0.075, hip 2210/720 and aiming 265.2/143.3 deg/s on the owner's Plutonium game) is kept, number for number, in dashboard/game-translators/cod-bo2-measured.json; BO2_USES_BO7 = false in that tool puts it back. BO2 stays Ready to use (its AI scripts). Owner 2026-10-16: BO2 runs its own measured shape (dead zone, curve) with XIM's OWN Black Ops 2 turn speeds per console (XIM game 4188: PlayStation 1013/264, Xbox 860.8/236.5 deg/s; PC takes PlayStation's). BO2_MODE in import-xim-translators.js switches between xim, bo7 and measured.",
